Given that $\alpha$ and $\beta(\alpha \gt \beta)$ are roots of the equation
$\begin{array}{ll} & x^2-8 x+q=0 \\ \therefore & \alpha+\beta=8 \text { and } \alpha \cdot \beta=q \\ \therefore & (\alpha-\beta)^2=(\alpha+\beta)^2-4 \alpha \beta=64-4 q \\ & (\alpha-\beta)=\sqrt{64-4 q} \quad(\because \alpha \gt \beta)\end{array}
$\begin{aligned} & \text { Now } \alpha^2-\beta^2=16 \\ \Rightarrow & (\alpha+\beta)-(\alpha-\beta)=16 \\ \Rightarrow & 8 \sqrt{64-4 q}=16 \quad \Rightarrow \sqrt{64-4 q}=2 \\ \Rightarrow & 64-4 q=4 \quad \Rightarrow q=15\end{aligned}

Given that $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are roots of the equation
$\begin{array}{cl} & x^2-a x-b x+a b-c=0 \\ \text { i.e } & x^2-(a+b) x+a b-c=0 \\ \therefore & \alpha+\beta=a+b \quad \alpha . \beta=a b-c \\ \Rightarrow & a b=\alpha \beta+c\end{array}
The quadratic equation whose roots are $a$ and $b$ is
$\begin{aligned} & x^2-(a+b) x+a b=0 \\ & x^2-(\alpha+\beta) x+\alpha \beta+c=0 \\ & x^2-\alpha x-\beta x+\alpha \beta+c=0\end{aligned}

$\frac{1}{\alpha^2}+\frac{1}{\beta^2} \lt 1 \Rightarrow \frac{\alpha^2+\beta^2}{(\alpha \beta)^2} \lt 1\begin{aligned}
& \Rightarrow \quad \frac{(\alpha+\beta)^2-2 \alpha \beta}{(\alpha \beta)^2} \lt 1 \\
& \alpha+\beta=\frac{-b}{a}=\left(1-2 \mathrm{a}^2\right) \& \alpha \beta=\frac{c}{a}=\left(1-2 \mathrm{a}^2\right) \\
& \text { On solving: } \quad \lt 1
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned}
& \frac{4 a^4-1}{4 a^4-4 a^2+1} \\
& \Rightarrow \quad 4 a^4-1 \lt 4 a^4-4 a^2+1 \\
& 4 a^2 \lt 2 \Rightarrow a^2 \lt \frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}$

