Let $\alpha, \beta$ be the roots of $x^2+\mathrm{bx}+\mathrm{c}=0$
Given, $\alpha+\beta=\frac{1}{\alpha^2}+\frac{1}{\beta^2}\begin{aligned}
& \alpha+\beta=-\mathrm{b} ; \alpha \cdot \beta=\mathrm{c} \\
& \therefore \alpha+\beta=\frac{1}{\alpha^2}+\frac{1}{\beta^2} \\
& \Rightarrow \alpha+\beta=\frac{\alpha^2}{\alpha^2}+\frac{\beta^2}{\beta^2}=\frac{(\alpha+\beta)^2-2 \alpha \beta}{\alpha^2 \beta^2}
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned}
& \Rightarrow-\mathrm{b}=\frac{\mathrm{b}^2-2 \mathrm{c}}{\mathrm{c}^2} \\
& \Rightarrow-\mathrm{bc}^2=\mathrm{b}^2-2 \mathrm{c} \Rightarrow 2 \mathrm{c}=\mathrm{b}^2+\mathrm{bc}^2 \\
& \Rightarrow \frac{2 \mathrm{c}}{\mathrm{~b}}=\mathrm{b}+\mathrm{c}^2 \Rightarrow \frac{2}{\mathrm{~b}}=\frac{\mathrm{b}}{\mathrm{c}}+\mathrm{c}
\end{aligned}$
We know, if $\mathrm{a}, \mathrm{b}, \mathrm{c}$ are in A.P. $2 \mathrm{~b}=\mathrm{a}+\mathrm{c}$.
Similarly, we got $\frac{2}{\mathrm{~b}}=\frac{\mathrm{b}}{\mathrm{c}}+\mathrm{c}$, which means $\frac{\mathrm{b}}{\mathrm{c}}, \frac{1}{\mathrm{~b}}, \mathrm{c}$ are in A.P.
So, $\frac{\mathrm{c}}{\mathrm{b}}, \mathrm{b}, \frac{1}{\mathrm{c}}$ are in H.P.

